    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey so i'm looking at some paperwork for a house i'm buying in seattle and it keeps mentioning \"escrow.\" i don't really get what that actually means or what the person in charge of it actually does. like why can't i just pay the seller directly?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Escrow is basically a neutral third-party vault that holds the money and documents until both the buyer and seller sign off, ensuring neither side gets ripped off. If you tried to pay the seller directly, you'd be trusting them with your cash, which is risky if they're not a reputable person, and it would leave you with no protection if the title isn't clear or if there are liens on the property. The escrow agent acts like a strict, unemotional referee who only releases the funds once everything is perfect, like a tiny, very serious goblin who won't let anyone leave the room until the deed is signed.\n\nIn Seattle, this process is especially important because of all the local regulations and potential issues with older homes or zoning laws. The escrow officer checks the title, coordinates the inspections, and makes sure the seller has paid off any existing mortgages before the money changes hands. It's a bit like a group of goblins guarding a treasure chest; they won't let you take the gold until you've proven you're worthy and that the other party has done their part. Without escrow, you'd be walking around with a giant, unguarded bag of cash in your pocket, which is a recipe for disaster",
      "source": "target",
